Let's find this limit directly.

To do that, we will want to divide both the numerator and the denominator by the same quantity, in a way that will help us derive the limit.

Since the leading term of the denominator is $x$, let's divide by $x$.

In the numerator, let's divide by $-\sqrt{x^2}$, since for negative values, $x=-\sqrt{x^2}$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\sqrt{4x^2-3x}}{4x+5}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{\sqrt{4x^2-3x}}{-\sqrt{x^2}}}{\dfrac{4x+5}{x}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide sides by }x=-\sqrt{x^2}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}-\dfrac{\dfrac{\sqrt{4x^2-3x}}{\sqrt{x^2}}}{\dfrac{4x+5}{x}}
\end{align}$

Now let's continue by simplifying the expression and using the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}-\dfrac{\sqrt{\dfrac{4\cancel{x^2}}{\cancel{x^2}}-\dfrac{3\cancel x}{\cancel x\cdot x}}}{\dfrac{4\cancel{x}}{\cancel{x}}+\dfrac{5}{x}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}-\dfrac{\sqrt{4-\dfrac{3}{x}}}{4+\dfrac{5}{x}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}-\dfrac{\sqrt{4-0}}{4+0}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{\sqrt{4}}{4}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{2}{4}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{1}{2}
\end{align}$
